Subject: Re: LotusPay -> Redwood handoff + MAP (SDK focus) — answers + code samples attached

Hi Samira, Rajesh, Elena —

Good questions. Short answers and attached examples. Full notes in the runbook attachment; below is a condensed actionable checklist you can hand to your engineers.

1) SDK distribution and reproducibility
- We publish SDKs to npm and PyPI (package names: @redwood/sdk, redwood-sdk). Each release is semver-tagged. For POC we recommend installing a release tag (e.g., 1.3.2) rather than rolling 'latest'.
- We attach a Dockerfile sample in redwood_sdk_examples_v1.zip. The repo also contains package-lock / poetry.lock snapshots to lock transitive deps.

2) Endpoint migration pattern (hosted -> dedicated)
- Phase A (Dual-write staging): Keep both hosted and dedicated endpoints active; write to hosted for read-after-write safety, mirror to dedicated for validation.
- Phase B (Client-side switch with graceful draining): Use feature-flagged client that reads an env var (REDWOOD_ENDPOINT). Add a short TTL on in-flight jobs and use idempotency tokens so retries against either endpoint are safe.
- Traffic cutover steps: update API key, verify 1000-consistent-sample job runs, flip env var in canary, monitor for 15m, then scale. Rollback: flip env var back if error rate > 1.5% over 5m.

3) Rate-limits and smoothing  (practical configs)
- Avoid synchronous 1000x small calls in a tight loop. Prefer batching (5–25 items per call) for reconciliation — our embeddings/generation endpoints support batch sizes that reduce tokens and request counts.
- Retry/backoff suggestion (default):
  • Retry policy: max_retries=4
  • Backoff: base=300ms, multiplier=2.0, jitter=0.2 (randomized)
  • Treat 429 and 5xx as retryable for idempotent calls; for non-idempotent, fail fast and surface to job orchestrator.
- Client-side leaky bucket: implement a short-term token bucket that paces to the rate-limit header (x-rw-limit-remaining/x-rw-limit-reset) returned by our API. Example pseudo-logic attached.

Inline Node.js sample (from attached examples) — safe retry wrapper:

const Redwood = require('@redwood/sdk');
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY, endpoint: process.env.REDWOOD_ENDPOINT });

async function safeCall(payload) {
  let attempts = 0;
  let backoff = 300;
  while (attempts <= 4) {
    try {
      return await client.generate(payload, { timeout: 30000 });
    } catch (err) {
      attempts += 1;
      if (!shouldRetry(err) || attempts > 4) throw err;
      await sleep(backoff * (1 + Math.random() * 0.2));
      backoff *= 2;
    }
  }
}

function shouldRetry(err) {
  if (err.statusCode >= 500) return true;
  if (err.statusCode === 429) return true;
  return false;
}

4) Observability hooks
- Include request_id and job_id in call metadata headers for tracing.
- Use our Console (link below) to watch per-key token usage and 429 occurrences during the cutover window.

